WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking with&nbsp;
Joe Henrich, who's professor of human evolutionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.120
biology at Harvard University and also author&nbsp;
of the book The Weirdest People in the World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.120
How the West Became Psychologically Peculiar and&nbsp;
Particularly Prosperous. It's really great having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.320
you on today. I appreciate your time.
Speaker 3: Yeah, good to be with.&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:28.000
Speaker 1: So let's first talk about Weird&nbsp;
is an acronym WTI, RDF for Western educated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.720
industrialized, rich and Democratic. So how did&nbsp;
you first start to sort of think about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.360
this group of people as a group of people and&nbsp;
sort of what the defining characteristics are?&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:47.440
Speaker 3: Yeah. So around 2006 or so, I&nbsp;
began working with two cultural psychologists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.560
Steve Hein in R and R in Xi'an at the University&nbsp;
of British Columbia. And, you know, we had come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.120
together over lunch and stuff and began to&nbsp;
talk about our research. And what we each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.480
had realized somewhat independently was not only&nbsp;
was there cultural variation around the world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.080
key aspects of psychology, but that the&nbsp;
populations most commonly studied by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.920
psychologists and others were unusual when seated&nbsp;
in that global perspective. So they were often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.880
at the tail end of the distribution or, you know,&nbsp;
distinctively set apart from other populations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.760
So we came up with the idea of writing a paper&nbsp;
together. The weirdest people in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.920
meant to raise an alarm, kind of a&nbsp;
methodological, methodological concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.920
aimed at psychologists and others that they&nbsp;
were over generalizing from the populations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.640
that they studied, that they they couldn't make&nbsp;
assumptions about human behavior by studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.480
American undergraduates or people&nbsp;
online, people from those sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.840
And then as a as a way of referring to the&nbsp;
populations most commonly studied by researchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.960
we came up with the acronym Weird just as way,&nbsp;
and it's meant as a consciousness raising device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.960
to kind of remind researchers that they&nbsp;
they should be cautious about generalizing.&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.880
Speaker 1: So when we talk&nbsp;
about the characteristics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:08.240
Western makes sense, educated makes sense,&nbsp;
industrialized, rich and democratic. When we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:15.360
talk about rich, are we talking about rich at a&nbsp;
global scale or are we saying even within these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.840
Western countries we're talking about the rich?
Speaker 3: Well, I mean, what we had in mind was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.240
mostly psychologists were sampling undergrad at&nbsp;
the time. They were mostly sampling undergraduates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:35.360
from a small handful of countries. So the US,&nbsp;
Canada, a couple of places in Europe, Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.240
places like that. So, you know, to the degree&nbsp;
to which American undergraduates tend to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.640
disproportionately sampled from the middle and&nbsp;
upper class. Yeah. So in that case it leaves out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.200
lower asks. And then of course, globally, those&nbsp;
are among the richest countries in the world.&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.520
Speaker 1: Before we move on to some of the&nbsp;
other aspects of your research, it's often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.320
repeated and things that are often repeated aren't&nbsp;
necessarily true, but it's often repeated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

