00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.600
unique and different, but it can also mean&nbsp;
that the sort of something maybe that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.200
would identify is stranger or unusual. What&nbsp;
are the psychological peculiarities of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.840
group that is often the source for for studies?
Speaker 3: Yeah. So the key thing I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.680
focus on is trying to understand the psychological&nbsp;
variation we find around across populations around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:34.160
the globe. So some of the key dimensions that&nbsp;
emerged were, one is a kind of psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.240
cluster or complex called individualism. That's&nbsp;
associated with how people think about themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.600
about overconfidence in self esteem&nbsp;
and the role of guilt versus shame.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.320
And so there's a whole cluster there where people&nbsp;
in weird societies tend to think about themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.800
as unitary individual decision makers rather&nbsp;
than nodes in a social network. They tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.880
be over. Are confident. They tend to have concerns&nbsp;
about guilt, but shame is relatively unimportant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.840
This goes along with another pattern which&nbsp;
is analytic versus holistic thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.760
So there tends to be an inclination towards more&nbsp;
analytic thinking. So breaking problems down into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:21.280
parts, assigning properties to those parts,&nbsp;
and then reasoning from that point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.880
as opposed to looking at the relationships among&nbsp;
parts to make explanations. So we find lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.480
variation there. The use of intentionality&nbsp;
and moral judgment to be another one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.480
So lots of Westerners really think intentions&nbsp;
are important. But in other places, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.240
intentions don't seem to be important at&nbsp;
all, at least in making some moral judgments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.920
Moral universalism. So the degree to which you&nbsp;
think everyone should be judged by the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.520
moral standards versus moral particularism, where&nbsp;
you make a difference between how people within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.760
some circle should be judged versus people&nbsp;
outside that circle. Trust in strangers is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.400
another important one. Things like that.
Speaker 1: What are the sources of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:08.480
peculiarities? I mean, is it is it media? Is&nbsp;
