00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:20.720
churches play a role? What what what causes this?
Speaker 3: Well, I mean, there's probably lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.640
factors that affect overall global psychological&nbsp;
variation. But in the book, I was particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.240
interested in trying to understand why it was that&nbsp;
European and European descent populations became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:39.680
particularly individualistic and particularly&nbsp;
have a strong, impersonal processing quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:45.500
So this trust in cooperation with strangers, which&nbsp;
should be contrasted with a more interpersonal pro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:52.160
sociology, where people are much more cooperative&nbsp;
and positively inclined towards people they know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.840
people they have relationships with,&nbsp;
that kind of thing with in-group loyalty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:04.480
So the the case that I make is that much of the&nbsp;
kind of unusual nature of European society as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:12.400
it emerged in the last 500 years, comes from&nbsp;
a particular set of taboos and prescriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.000
that one branch of Christianity, the&nbsp;
one that evolves into the Roman Catholic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:23.840
Church, began to promulgate in late antiquity and&nbsp;
eventually, although gradually spreads throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:30.560
many parts of Western Europe and transforms the&nbsp;
intensive, complex kinship organizations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.280
you would find in many pre-Christian European&nbsp;
societies and breaks them down into monogamous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.640
tubular families. And then this opens&nbsp;
the door for other kinds of institutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:46.080
impersonal institutions, eventually things&nbsp;
like democracy and Western law and whatnot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.920
But initially things like guilds, universities,&nbsp;
monasteries that were organized differently and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:57.120
built around notions of individuals trying to&nbsp;
attract individuals. So it really puts these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.480
populations on a different kind of cultural&nbsp;
evolutionary trajectory than we we see in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.560
societies around the world and back into history.
Speaker 1: So if I understand correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:12.800
even though today in the United States, for&nbsp;
example, Roman Catholics are not a majority, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.080
evolution and growth of the Roman Catholic&nbsp;
Church, starting 500 years ago in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:25.120
led to, in a sense, psychological changes,&nbsp;
which include acceptance of certain types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.160
of relationships to institutions that&nbsp;
are still very relevant today. In terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.560
of how this group you study relates to the world.
Speaker 3: That's right. You should think of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:40.000
the stuff the Catholic Church did as initiating&nbsp;
a kind of cultural evolutionary process. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:46.000
these principles get to varying degrees embedded&nbsp;
in secular institutions and become part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:51.440
general cultural milieu. One of the one of the&nbsp;
cases I make in the book is that this practice led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:57.760
to the emergence of Protestantism. So, you know,&nbsp;
there's this goes on for a thousand years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:03.120
Martin Luther and others initiate this&nbsp;
Protestant reformation, which, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.760
is a is a constellation, a mosaic of different&nbsp;
religious movements that are rebelling against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.480
the Catholic Church. But, I mean, they&nbsp;
have sort of super weird characteristics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.920
in the sense that they're really focused on&nbsp;
mental states, the role of intentionality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:23.200
sensuality on the individual, emphasis&nbsp;
on hard work, deferral of gratification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.680
So it is only further accentuates some&nbsp;
of the basic characteristics that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:31.920
