of our psychology are adapted to the institutional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:48.080
and ecological environments that we have to&nbsp;
confront. So they did have Chris Blackmon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:54.080
colleagues, political scientists and economists.&nbsp;
The University of Chicago did this research in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.080 --> 00:16:00.149
Liberia, where they they basically created a&nbsp;
training course to try to increase the self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.149 --> 00:16:05.120
regulation of poor men. They try to make them more&nbsp;
patient and more willing to defer gratification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:10.720
The training worked and they were able&nbsp;
to show that on the psychological tests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:16.720
men were willing to defer gratification more.&nbsp;
And initially, there seemed to be a big success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:21.200
because the men were able to begin to develop&nbsp;
little nest eggs of savings and things like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.240
Right. Okay. So that's working. But then it turns&nbsp;
out that because the environment is so unstable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:30.000
the men would get robbed or they'd get shaken&nbsp;
down by the police or something would happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.800
and they'd lose their complete nest egg so they&nbsp;
wouldn't get the pleasure that would have come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:40.880
from indulging in that. So that that's one way.&nbsp;
And you need a stable environment where you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:45.360
preserve that savings and you need the chance that&nbsp;
you're going to be alive when you get a chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:50.320
take advantage of it. Otherwise it's better to to&nbsp;
consume now when you're sure you're going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:55.760
something out of it. The other thing that that&nbsp;
varies across societies is in a lot of cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:02.960
if I accumulate much, my my relatives are going&nbsp;
to come and ask for it, essentially. So we'll die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:09.920
There'll be a funeral or there'll be a wedding. So&nbsp;
what I do one way to deal with any kind of rising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:15.200
surplus is just to give it away, have a party&nbsp;
or give it to others. And the way people think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:20.240
about that, or maybe not consciously, but the way&nbsp;
it ends up functioning, is that you're storing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:25.840
your surplus in the goodwill and relationships&nbsp;
you have with others. Right? When you're in need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:29.040
you don't have to have a nest egg. Your&nbsp;
nest egg is scattered among all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:34.000
friends and relatives and close contacts that&nbsp;
you've cultivated over long periods of time.&nbsp;

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.280
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's super interesting.&nbsp;
And to some degree, I've studied that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:43.040
little bit in Hasidic Jewish communities,&nbsp;
and there's that that phenomena is very, very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:48.400
notable there. Interestingly enough, the book is&nbsp;
fascinating. It's called The Weirdest People in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:52.960
the World How the West Became Psychologically&nbsp;
Peculiar and Particularly Prosperous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:57.120
We've been speaking with the book's author,&nbsp;
Harvard University professor Joe Henrich.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:01.200
Joe, really appreciate your time today.
Speaker 3: Okay. Well, it's great to be with you.

